# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b1), edited. myrtle_somerton's first conversation.

~ start
set myrtle_somerton_met
Narrator: Smelling of starch and chamomile, she folds her hands over her apron and glances toward the nursery stairs.
myrtle_somerton [neutral]: Wendel is in the office, though please keep your voices low near little Rose's door.
* Ask after her family and daily life -> about_work
* Ask how she finds life in Wickermoor -> about_village
* Ask if the neighbors have been well -> rumour
* [Insight DC 13] Notice the tension in her hands when she mentions the children -> secret_ok | secret_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ about_work
myrtle_somerton [weary]: I keep the silver bright and stop Wendel promising the parish away to flatter quality, but my real work is keeping Rose indoors at her lessons.
-> menu

~ about_village
myrtle_somerton [neutral]: Folk should mind their own hearths. I tell Wendel this fuss over old trees and midnight bells only gives simple folk bad dreams.
-> menu

~ rumour
myrtle_somerton [sad]: Too many shutters stay barred past the third bell, and no broth touches the rattle in the baker's apprentice's chest.
-> menu

~ secret_ok
Narrator: Her knuckles whiten on her apron hem, and you see iron pins driven into the windowsill beside a child's wooden doll.
myrtle_somerton [afraid]: Folk who know the paths from birth do not just wander into the gorse, whatever the constable writes. I bolt Rose's shutters before the first bell for good.
-> menu

~ secret_fail
Narrator: She smooths her skirt with a brisk pat and offers a tight, proper nod.
myrtle_somerton [neutral]: A mother is always careful in damp weather, that is all. The moor air is hard on small lungs.
-> menu

~ menu
